    "curve_shape": "CLIFF_THEN_PLATEAU -- a sharp drop from t=1 to t<1 (the head IS hurt by noise) followed by a non-monotonic plateau around the post-cliff value. ARCH-1's 'monotonic decrease' prediction is REFUTED on the load-bearing half: the head's quality stops depending on t once the geometry is sufficiently off-clean. A pure ARCH-1 retrain (noising the input) might raise the post-cliff floor but cannot recover the t=1 regime from noisy x_t.",
    "t1_macro_f1": 0.6108482787269279,
    "t1_macro_f1_vs_baseline": {
      "baseline_mean": 0.4784,
      "baseline_std": 0.0055,
      "abs_diff": 0.13244827872692794,
      "consistent_within_4std": false,
      "explanation": "Protocol difference, NOT a finding about the curve. The baseline (metrics/by_metric/tmqm_bond_perception_3seed.json) is macro_f1_type OVER SUCCESSES -- a TYPE-only macro-F1 that EXCLUDES BOND_NO_BOND. The script's macro_f1_all_classes averages over ALL 5 classes INCLUDING BOND_NO_BOND, which is the easy class with the largest support (2614 of 3770 pairs at t=1) and the head classifies it well (F1 0.95). Including the easy class inflates the macro average relative to a type-only average. Additionally, the baseline uses 900 complexes (150 per metal, 3 seeds); this run uses 32 mixed-metal clouds and 1 seed. A higher t=1 number is the direction the protocol difference predicts; the gap is expected."
    },
    "verdict_label": "ARCH-1 PARTIALLY REFUTED. The head IS hurt by noise (sharp cliff from t=1 to t=0.9) but the post-cliff floor does not depend on t, so the strict-monotonic prediction fails. The retrain slot should NOT be spent on ARCH-1 alone -- the cliff-then-plateau shape suggests a capacity floor not bridged by noising the input."
